              "ansi": "  Based on that, here's the likely failure: someone changes a role in the UI, then mirrors the change into \u001b[0m\u001b[38;2;177;185;249minfra-config.yml\u001b[0m. Plan without refresh compares the new config against the old state and wants to replace the user. \u001b[0m\u001b[38;2;177;185;249mprevent_destroy \u001b[0mthen makes \u001b[0m\u001b[1mplan error out\u001b[0m. A\r\n  refreshed plan would pull the new role into state and show a no-op.\r\n\r\n  Revised round. Earlier answers still welcome.\r\n\r\n  ❓ \u001b[0m\u001b[1mQ1 - Failure sequence\u001b[0m: Does the failing plan follow this sequence: change the role in the UI, update \u001b[0m\u001b[38;2;177;185;249minfra-config.yml \u001b[0mto match, then plan errors with \u001b[0m\u001b[38;2;177;185;249mInstance cannot be destroyed \u001b[0mfrom \u001b[0m\u001b[38;2;177;185;249mprevent_destroy\u001b[0m?\r\n\r\n  ➡️ Yes. If the config is \u001b[0m\u001b[3mnot \u001b[0mupdated, plan shows a no-op and the next apply fails instead, because apply refreshes and sees drift.\r\n\r\n  ---\r\n\r\n  ❓ \u001b[0m\u001b[1mQ2 - Fields changed in the UI\u001b[0m: Is \u001b[0m\u001b[38;2;177;185;249muser_role \u001b[0mthe only field you change in the UI? Email, groups and deletion would stay in Terraform.\r\n\r\n  ➡️ Yes.\r\n\r\n  ---\r\n\r\n  ❓ \u001b[0m\u001b[1mQ3 - Config mirrors UI\u001b[0m: After a UI role change, should \u001b[0m\u001b[38;2;177;185;249minfra-config.yml \u001b[0mstill be updated to the new role, so the config documents who has which role?\r\n\r\n  ➡️ Yes. The config is the access record; the UI is only the means of making the change. Then \u001b[0m\u001b[38;2;177;185;249m-refresh=true \u001b[0mon plan is enough, and \u001b[0m\u001b[38;2;177;185;249mignore_changes \u001b[0mwould hide config/UI mismatches.\r\n\r\n  The alternative is (b): the config holds only the \u001b[0m\u001b[3minitial \u001b[0mrole, the UI owns it afterwards, and you add \u001b[0m\u001b[38;2;177;185;249mignore_changes = [user_role]\u001b[0m. That needs no flag, but the config drifts from reality.\r\n\r\n  ---\r\n\r\n  ❓ \u001b[0m\u001b[1mQ4 - Scope\u001b[0m: Should only the \u001b[0m\u001b[38;2;177;185;249mapplication_layer/quicksight/access_management/users \u001b[0mtarget get the refreshed plan?\r\n\r\n  ➡️ Yes.\r\n\r\n  ---\r\n\r\n  ❓ \u001b[0m\u001b[1mQ5 - Other plan pipelines\u001b[0m: Do the Jenkins \u001b[0m\u001b[38;2;177;185;249mdeploy-ci/lens/plan/* \u001b[0mjobs, which are not in this repo, plan the users target with a role that may lack \u001b[0m\u001b[38;2;177;185;249mquicksight:DescribeUser\u001b[0m?\r\n\r\n  ➡️ Unknown to me, so I recommend checking. Only labs-data prd defines \u001b[0m\u001b[38;2;177;185;249mquicksight_v2\u001b[0m, and it plans with the same role as apply. If that's the only environment that matters, the risk is low.\r\n\r\n\u001b[0m\u001b[38;2;78;186;101m⏺ \u001b[0mAgent \"Find QuickSight users facts\" finished\u001b[0m\u001b[38;2;153;153;153m · 1m 4s\u001b[0m\r\n\r\n\u001b[0m\u001b[38;2;153;153;153m※ \u001b[0m\u001b[1m\u001b[38;2;153;153;153mrecap: \u001b[0m\u001b[3m\u001b[38;2;153;153;153mWe're deciding whether the QuickSight users target should plan with refresh enabled, since prevent_destroy likely makes stale plans error after UI role changes. Next, answer the five grilling questions above, starting with whether that failure sequence \u001b[0m\r\n  \u001b[0m\u001b[3m\u001b[38;2;153;153;153mmatches what you see. (disable recaps in /config)\u001b[0m\r\n\r\n\u001b[0m\u001b[38;2;136;136;136m────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\u001b[0m\r\n❯ \r\n\u001b[0m\u001b[38;2;136;136;136m─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────